{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And he called his name Jacob.</b> The letters of יעקב are four, corresponding to the four crowns that his sons are destined to crown the Holy One, blessed be He. Yod corresponds to the Ten Commandments; ayin corresponds to the seventy elders; kuf corresponds to the Hall that they built for the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is said of it: וההיכל מאה אמה; bet corresponds to the two tablets. And in the merit of Jacob, Israel were redeemed from Egypt, as it is said: ויעמידה ליעקב לחק לישראל etc. And were it not for Jacob, Abraham would not have been redeemed from the furnace, as it is said: כה אמר ה׳ אל בית יעקב אשר פדה את אברהם. Another interpretation: our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded that he redeemed him from the distress of raising children."], :book :genesis, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The last line ('Another interpretation: he redeemed him from the distress of raising children') — the subject is ambiguous; read hyper-literally. The citation from Jeremiah is rendered as it stands with the divine name in Hebrew script as it appears in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:06:01.090054474Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}